<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jiajing Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1507">1507</a>–1567</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Zhu Houcong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jiajing Emperor, personal name Zhu Houcong, was the 12th emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, reigning from 1521 to 1567. A cousin of the Zhengde Emperor, who died without an heir, he came to the throne unexpectedly and at once clashed with his officials in the Great Rites Controversy, from which he emerged victorious after three years. From 1542 he lived in the West Park outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a> among Taoist temples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> and trusted advisers, and his pursuit of immortality led him to elixirs containing arsenic, lead and mercury. His reign saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altan-khan">Altan Khan</a>'s raids, which reached the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1550, and wokou piracy that peaked in the 1550s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jiajing-emperor.jpg" alt="Jiajing Emperor" width="330" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_officiel_de_l'empereur_Jiajing.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhu Houcong was born on 16 September 1507, the eldest son of Zhu Youyuan, Prince of Xing, a fourth son of the Chenghua Emperor known for his poetry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>. His parents had lived since 1494 at Anlu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguang">Huguang</a>, in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, where he was born. When the Zhengde Emperor died on 20 April 1521, Grand Secretary Yang Tinghe, with the support of court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> and Empress Dowager Zhang, issued an edict calling the prince to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> to take the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The new emperor wished to raise his father posthumously to imperial rank, while Yang Tinghe insisted that he be formally adopted by the Hongzhi Emperor and so become the younger brother of the late Zhengde Emperor. The emperor and his mother rejected the adoption, and he did not want to declare his parents his uncle and aunt. After three years of the Great Rites Controversy he emerged victorious, and his main opponents were banished or executed. In the late 1530s he introduced separate sacrifices to the Heavens, Earth, Sun and Moon. He brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> from Anlu to fill palace posts, and their influence kept growing.</p><p>On 27 November 1542 a group of palace women tried to strangle him with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> cord. He lay unconscious for eight hours, and Empress Fang had the women executed. He moved that year to the West Park, where he built palaces and Taoist temples and was served by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>, Taoist monks and advisers such as Zhang Cong, Xia Yan, Yan Song and Xu Jie. His pursuit of immortality led to elixirs holding arsenic, lead and mercury.</p><p>In the 1540s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altan-khan">Altan Khan</a> united the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and demanded the restoration of free trade; the emperor refused, and in 1550 Altan's raids reached the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. Wokou piracy reached its peak in the 1550s. The deadly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1556-shaanxi-earthquake">1556 Shaanxi earthquake</a> struck, yet agriculture, industry and trade continued to grow, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-yangming">Wang Yangming</a>'s more individual teaching gained ground over the traditional Confucian interpretation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the 1560s the emperor suffered from the effects of the potions, insomnia and mood swings, and after 11 November 1566 he issued no written orders because of illness. On 23 January 1567 he moved from the West Park to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a>, where he died around midday. He was buried in the Yong Mausoleum in the Ming tombs near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and received the temple name Shizong.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jiajing Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The early Ming emperors favoured Taoism and gave its practitioners many posts in the state’s ritual offices, and the <b>Jiajing Emperor</b> (r. 1521–1567) persecuted Buddhism at court in favour of Taoism.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zhongxiang City, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.17,112.59&amp;year=1546" aria-label="Zhongxiang City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jiajing Emperor"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4459 4883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4459 4883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.17,112.59&amp;year=1546">What was here in 1546? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
